WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.810
Task statement 5.6.

2
00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:07.393
Preserve information provenance and handle uncertainty in multi-source synthesis.

3
00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:16.905
When two credible sources give different market sizes, a research report that quietly averages them is wrong in a way no reader can check.

4
00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:24.177
When several sources are merged into one answer, the first thing lost is usually who said what.

5
00:00:24.177 --> 00:00:30.111
Once a claim loses its source, nobody can check it, weigh it, or spot when two sources disagree.

6
00:00:30.111 --> 00:00:32.398
Think of footnotes in a good article.

7
00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:37.591
Every claim carries its return address, and its date, all the way to the final page.

8
00:00:38.991 --> 00:00:40.994
Make provenance part of the data.

9
00:00:40.994 --> 00:00:47.852
Each subagent returns claims as small records with the source, the publication date and a short supporting quote.

10
00:00:47.852 --> 00:00:55.075
The synthesis step keeps those records attached to each statement instead of rewriting everything into unsourced prose.

11
00:00:55.075 --> 00:01:02.116
If you use the Claude API directly, the citations feature can point each sentence to the exact passage it came from.

12
00:01:03.516 --> 00:01:08.068
When credible sources disagree, show both values and say who reported each.

13
00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:11.103
Do not average them and do not quietly choose one.

14
00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:16.687
Check the dates first, because many apparent contradictions are just different time periods.

15
00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:25.852
Then present established findings separately from contested ones, and pick a fitting format, such as a table for figures and prose for the explanation.

16
00:01:27.252 --> 00:01:32.959
In the research scenario, one source says heat-pump sales fell and another says installs rose.

17
00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:38.303
Check dates and definitions first, since sales and installations are different measures.

18
00:01:38.303 --> 00:01:40.974
Put agreed points in an established section.

19
00:01:40.974 --> 00:01:46.378
Put the disagreement in a contested section with both figures, each attributed and dated.

20
00:01:46.378 --> 00:01:50.143
Use a table for the numbers and a short paragraph for caveats.

21
00:01:51.543 --> 00:01:55.388
Distractors here make the report look neat at the cost of truth.

22
00:01:55.388 --> 00:02:01.877
Averaging, silently picking the newest number, and flattening everything into prose all destroy information.

23
00:02:01.877 --> 00:02:10.529
The exam's answers keep sources attached, show conflicts with attribution and dates, and present each kind of content in a format that suits it.

24
00:02:11.929 --> 00:02:12.472
To recap.

25
00:02:12.472 --> 00:02:18.868
Provenance survives only if it is structured, so carry claim, source and date together through every step.

26
00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:30.212
Present disagreements openly with attribution, use dates to explain apparent conflicts, separate what is settled from what is contested, and choose a format that fits each type of content.

